### What is the Longest Common Subsequence?

The Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) of two sequences is the longest sequence of elements that appears in both inputs in the same relative order, without requiring the elements to be contiguous. For strings "ABCBDAB" and "BDCAB", one LCS is "BCAB" with length 4.

LCS is the foundation of diff tools (showing what changed between file versions), DNA sequence alignment, and version comparison.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LcsBasic {

    public static int lcs(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length(), n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;  // match: extend diagonal
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);  // drop one side
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m][n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("LCS length: " + lcs("ABCBDAB", "BDCAB"));
    }
}
```

```text
LCS length: 4
```

### How does the DP grid work?

The grid has (m + 1) rows and (n + 1) columns. Cell dp[i][j] stores the LCS length of the first i characters of A and the first j characters of B. The first row and first column are all zeros because the LCS of any string with an empty string is zero.

When A[i-1] equals B[j-1], the current characters match, so we extend the best LCS up to the previous characters: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1. When they differ, we cannot use both current characters, so we take the better of dropping the last character from A (dp[i-1][j]) or dropping the last from B (dp[i][j-1]).

### How do you reconstruct the actual LCS?

Walk backward from dp[m][n]. If A[i-1] == B[j-1], include this character and move diagonally. Otherwise, move in the direction of the larger neighbour (up or left).

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LcsReconstruct {

    public static String findLCS(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length(), n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
                dp[i][j] = a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)
                    ? dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
                    : Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);

        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        int i = m, j = n;
        while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
            if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                sb.append(a.charAt(i - 1));
                i--; j--;
            } else if (dp[i - 1][j] > dp[i][j - 1]) {
                i--;
            } else {
                j--;
            }
        }
        return sb.reverse().toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("LCS: " + findLCS("ABCBDAB", "BDCAB"));
    }
}
```

```text
LCS: BCAB
```

### How do you optimise space with rolling rows?

Each row depends only on the previous row, so keep two arrays and alternate. This reduces space from O(m * n) to O(min(m, n)). However, reconstruction is lost because we no longer have the full grid.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LcsRolling {

    public static int lcs(String a, String b) {
        if (a.length() < b.length()) return lcs(b, a);  // shorter string as columns
        int n = b.length();
        int[] prev = new int[n + 1];
        int[] curr = new int[n + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= a.length(); i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    curr[j] = prev[j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    curr[j] = Math.max(prev[j], curr[j - 1]);
                }
            }
            int[] temp = prev; prev = curr; curr = temp;
            java.util.Arrays.fill(curr, 0);
        }
        return prev[n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("LCS length: " + lcs("ABCBDAB", "BDCAB"));
    }
}
```

```text
LCS length: 4
```

### How is LCS different from Longest Common Substring?

| Aspect | LCS (Subsequence) | Longest Common Substring |
|---|---|---|
| Contiguity | Not required | Must be contiguous |
| Match rule | dp[i-1][j-1] + 1 | dp[i-1][j-1] + 1 |
| Mismatch rule | max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]) | 0 (reset) |
| Answer location | dp[m][n] | max value in entire table |
| Time | O(m * n) | O(m * n) |

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LongestCommonSubstring {

    public static int longestSubstring(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length(), n = b.length(), best = 0;
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                    best = Math.max(best, dp[i][j]);
                }
                // else dp[i][j] remains 0
            }
        return best;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Longest common substring: " + longestSubstring("ABCBDAB", "BDCAB"));
    }
}
```

```text
Longest common substring: 2
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is confusing LCS (subsequence, non-contiguous) with longest common substring (contiguous). The mismatch handling is completely different: LCS takes the max of two neighbours, while substring resets to zero.

Another mistake is indexing: dp[i][j] compares A[i-1] with B[j-1], not A[i] with B[j]. Off-by-one errors cause wrong results or ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.

### What are the edge cases?

When one string is empty, the LCS length is zero. When both strings are identical, the LCS is the string itself. When the strings share no characters, the LCS is zero.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LcsEdge {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(LcsBasic.lcs("", "ABC"));        // 0
        System.out.println(LcsBasic.lcs("ABC", "ABC"));     // 3
        System.out.println(LcsBasic.lcs("ABC", "XYZ"));     // 0
    }
}
```

