0
3
0
```

### When should you use LCS in banking?

At the National Bank of India, the reconciliation team compares transaction logs from the core banking system and the payment gateway. The LCS reveals the longest ordered set of matching transactions, highlighting what was inserted or deleted between the two feeds.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TransactionReconciler {

    public static int matchingTransactions(String[] coreLog, String[] gatewayLog) {
        int m = coreLog.length, n = gatewayLog.length;
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
                dp[i][j] = coreLog[i - 1].equals(gatewayLog[j - 1])
                    ? dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
                    : Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
        return dp[m][n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] core = {"TXN001", "TXN002", "TXN003", "TXN005"};
        String[] gateway = {"TXN001", "TXN003", "TXN004", "TXN005"};
        System.out.println("Matching transactions: " + matchingTransactions(core, gateway));
    }
}
```

```text
Matching transactions: 3
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that LCS connects to edit distance: when only insert and delete are allowed (no replace), the edit distance is m + n - 2 * LCS(A, B). This relationship helps verify solutions.

For very long sequences, Hirschberg's algorithm computes the LCS in O(m * n) time with only O(min(m, n)) space while still reconstructing the actual subsequence, using a divide-and-conquer approach on the rolling rows.

The Hunt-Szymanski algorithm is faster than O(m * n) when the number of matching character pairs is small, which is common in diff tools where most lines match.
